  "result": "The gate returned **OK** on the first run (1 of 6 used): 3 witnesses, 2 names, 2 atoms read.\n\n**Body** (`library/TrafficSpec/Concepts/motion/parked.lean`):\n```lean\n.holdsFor .time (.param \"min_duration\") \"a\"\n  (.derived \"drv_motion_complete_halt\" [.var \"a\"] [.param \"speed_tol\"])\n```\nThis says road user a is at rest, meaning its speed is at or below `speed_tol` through the accepted `drv.motion.complete_halt`, at every observation of the `min_duration` window behind. The result is unknown if that window reaches before the recording starts or a speed in it is missing, as the contract says. I declared no narrowings. The contract gives `min_duration` in seconds, so a time window is its own wording, not a choice I made.\n\n**Witnesses** (`library/TrafficSpecWitness/Concepts/motion/parked.lean`):\n- **Setup:** `speed_tol` is 0.1 m/s and `min_duration` is 2 s. Each trace has six scenes, one per second. Each scenario is judged only from t = 3000 to 5000 ms, so every 2 s window falls inside the trace.\n- **holds:** the bicycle's speed is 0 throughout, so the result is compliant.\n- **fails:** the bicycle rides at 2 m/s, stops at t = 2000 and 3000 ms, then rides off. The window ending at 3000 ms includes the 2 m/s at 1000 ms, so the result is violated.\n- **blind:** the same as holds with `kin.speed` masked, so the result is unknown.\n\n`task_result.md` is written in the working directory.",
